 Developer Tools\nL85: \nL86: #### Open-sourcing new AI tools for adaptive experimentation\nL87: \nL88: We are open-sourcing BoTorch and Ax, two new tools that leverage adaptive experimentation to efficiently tackle optimization challenges in tuning software configurations, machine learning hyperparameters, ranking systems, and more.\nL89: \nL90: May 01, 2019\nL91: \nL92: cite23†Learn More L93: \nL94: Blog\nL95: \nL96: October 24, 2025\nL97: \nL98: #### Open Source\nL99: #### The Building Blocks of Agentic AI: From Kernels to Clusters\nL100: \nL101: At PyTorch Conference 2025 in San Francisco, we unveiled five new projects spanning kernel languages, distributed systems, reinforcement learning, agentic frameworks, and edge AI deployment.\nL102: \nL103: October 24, 2025\nL104: \nL105: cite24†Learn More L106: \nL107: Blog\nL108: \nL109: August 05, 2024\nL110: \nL111: #### Open Source\nL112: #### Call for Applications: Llama 3.1 Impact Grants\nL113: \nL114: We’re excited to begin accepting applications for the Llama 3.1 Impact Grants, the next iteration of a larger portfolio of work to support organizations as they pursue their ideas for how Llama can be used to address social challenges in their communities.\nL115: \nL116: August 05, 2024\nL117: \nL118: cite25†Learn More L119: \nL120: Blog\nL121: \nL122: September 25, 2024\nL123: \nL124: #### Open Source\nL125: #### Connect 2024: The responsible approach we’re taking to generative AI\nL126: \nL127: With the rapidly evolving AI landscape, we recognize the importance of sharing our responsibility and safety approach with everyone.\nL128: \nL129: September 25, 2024\nL130: \nL131: cite26†Learn More L132: \nL133: Blog\nL134: \nL135: September 25, 2024\nL136: \nL137: #### Developer Tools\nL138: \nL139: #### Open Source\nL140: #### Llama 3.2: Revolutionizing edge AI and vision with open, customizable models\nL141: \nL142: Today, we’re releasing Llama 3.2, which includes small and medium-sized vision LLMs, and lightweight, text-only models that fit onto edge and mobile devices.\nL143: \nL144: September 25, 2024\nL145: \nL146: cite27†Learn More L147: \nL148: Blog\nL149: \nL150: April 29, 2025\nL151: \nL152: #### Developer Tools\nL153: \nL154: #### Open Source\nL155: #### Sharing new open source protection tools and advancements in AI privacy and security\nL156: \nL157: Today, we’re releasing new Llama protection tools for the open source AI community.\nL158: \nL159: April 29, 2025\nL160: \nL161: cite28†Learn More L162: \nL163: Blog\nL164: \nL165: August 27, 2025\nL166: \nL167: #### Open Source\nL168: #### How Llama and Oracle are helping Instituto PROA kickstart careers for students in Brazil\nL169: \nL170: Instituto PROA, a nonprofit organization in Brazil, has transformed its job preparation process for young candidates by leveraging Llama and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure.\nL171: \nL172: August 27, 2025\nL173: \nL174: cite29†Learn More L175: \nL176: Blog\nL177: \nL178: August 14, 2025\nL179: \nL180: #### Open Source\nL181: #### DINOv3: Self-supervised learning for vision at unprecedented scale\nL182: \nL183: DINOv3 scales self-supervised learning for images to create universal vision backbones that achieve absolute state-of-the-art performance across diverse domains, including web and satellite imagery.\nL184: \nL185: August 14, 2025\nL186: \nL187: cite30†Learn More L188: \nL189: Blog\nL190: \nL191: August 14, 2025\nL192: \nL193: #### Open Source\nL194: #### Small robots, mighty vision: NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory's DINOv2-enabled robot rovers and the future of planetary exploration\nL195: \nL196: Using DINOv2, the team at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory built a convenient robot operating system interface for robotic tasks.\nL197: \nL198: August 14, 2025\nL199: \nL200: cite31†Learn More L201: \nL202: Blog\nL203: \nL204: August 14, 2025\nL205: \nL206: #### Research\nL207: #### How DINOv3 is helping World Resources Institute restore forests and farms globally