aces in the United Kingdom with DINO\nL124: \nL125: Meta's DINOv2 model is enhancing reforestation efforts around the world. Learn how the UK government is using DINO to help reduce costs and increase access to greenspaces.\nL126: \nL127: February 09, 2026\nL128: \nL129: cite26†Learn More L130: \nL131: Blog\nL132: \nL133: February 20, 2025\nL134: \nL135: #### Open Source\nL136: #### How Orakl Oncology is using DINOv2 to accelerate cancer treatment discovery\nL137: \nL138: Orakl Oncology aims to accelerate cancer research and drug development by combining experimental, lab-based insights and machine learning.\nL139: \nL140: February 20, 2025\nL141: \nL142: cite27†Learn More L143: \nL144: Blog\nL145: \nL146: December 18, 2025\nL147: \nL148: #### Computer Vision\nL149: #### The Universities Space Research Association Applies Segment Anything Model for Responding to Flood Emergencies\nL150: \nL151: The Universities Space Research Association and Meta are collaborating to help support water observing systems set up by the U.S. Geological Survey.\nL152: \nL153: December 18, 2025\nL154: \nL155: cite28†Learn More L156: \nL157: Blog\nL158: \nL159: December 16, 2025\nL160: \nL161: #### Computer Vision\nL162: #### Introducing SAM Audio: The First Unified Multimodal Model for Audio Separation\nL163: \nL164: SAM Audio transforms audio processing by making it easy to isolate any sound from complex audio mixtures using natural, multimodal prompts — whether through text, visual cues, or marking time segments.\nL165: \nL166: December 16, 2025\nL167: \nL168: cite29†Learn More L169: \nL170: Blog\nL171: \nL172: November 19, 2025\nL173: \nL174: #### Computer Vision\nL175: #### Introducing SAM 3D: Powerful 3D Reconstruction for Physical World Images\nL176: \nL177: This release introduces two new state-of-the-art models: SAM 3D Objects for object and scene reconstruction, and SAM 3D Body for human body and shape estimation.\nL178: \nL179: November 19, 2025\nL180: \nL181: cite